================================================================================

STANDARD OPERATING PROCEDURE

Automated Downloads Folder Cleanup

Targeting Standalone Public Workstations | Windows 10/11 Pro & Enterprise

================================================================================



1. PURPOSE & OVERVIEW

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

This Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) details the exact steps required to 

automatically purge patron files and subfolders from the Downloads directory 

upon system reboot or user logon. To preserve workstation stability and maintain 

user shell structure, commands target file contents recursively without removing 

system shell folders or corrupting profile environment variables.



Why This Method is Safe for Public Library PCs:

* Preserves Shell Directories: Clears all subfiles and subfolders within 

  Downloads without deleting the root Downloads folder itself.

* Prevents Profile Corruption: Avoids stripping hidden system attributes or 

  disrupting user profile environment variables.

* Silent Background Execution: Runs completely in the background 

  (-WindowStyle Hidden) without flashing command prompts to patrons.





2. TASK SCHEDULER SETUP (RECOMMENDED)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Follow these step-by-step instructions on each standalone library computer to 

schedule automatic folder cleanup.



[Step 1: Open Tool]

- Press Win + R, type taskschd.msc, and press Enter.



[Step 2: Create Task]

- In the right-hand Actions panel, click Create Task...



[Step 3: General Tab]

- Name: Library Downloads Cleanup

- Check: Run with highest privileges (Required for full file access).



[Step 4: Triggers Tab]

- Click New...

- Set Begin the task to: At startup (or At log on).

- Click OK.



[Step 5: Actions Tab]

- Click New...

- Action: Start a program

- Program/script: powershell.exe

- Add arguments: See command below in Section 3.

- Click OK.



[Step 6: Finalize]

- Click OK to save the task. Test by rebooting the workstation or logging off 

  and back in.





3. COMMAND ARGUMENTS FOR STEP 5

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

In Step 5 of the Task Scheduler setup, enter the following exact string in the 

'Add arguments (optional)' box:



-NoProfile -NonInteractive -WindowStyle Hidden -Command "Get-ChildItem -Path '$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads' -Recurse -Force | Remove-Item -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue"





4. OPTIONAL GROUP POLICY (BYPASS RECYCLE BIN)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If you prefer that files deleted manually by patrons instantly bypass the 

Recycle Bin altogether at all times:



1. Press Win + R, type gpedit.msc, and press Enter.

2. Navigate to: User Configuration > Administrative Templates > Windows Components > File Explorer

3. Double-click policy: "Do not move deleted files to the Recycle Bin"

4. Set to Enabled and click OK.



Note: If you only want cleanup to happen automatically at startup/logon via 

Task Scheduler, keep this Group Policy set to Not Configured.

